          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A note says hypertension and chronic kidney disease. Does the classification assume a relationship between them, and what changes if it does?",
          "answer": "Yes. For hypertension and chronic kidney disease, the coding classification assumes a link between them unless the record says otherwise.\n\n**The assumption.** The US clinical coding guide for hypertension says the classification \"presumes a causal link between hypertension and chronic kidney disease and pairs them without being told to.\" This is in the coding document \"Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family.\" The same guide says it makes no such assumption for heart disease. For heart disease, the record must state the link before the codes are combined.\n\n**What changes.** When a record lists both conditions, the coder uses a combination code instead of the plain hypertension code (I10). According to the same document:\n- Hypertension with chronic kidney disease moves to **I12**, with a second code for the stage of kidney disease.\n- Hypertension with both heart disease and kidney disease moves to **I13**.\n- Hypertension with no organ involvement stays at **I10**.\n\nThe kidney stage code comes from the genitourinary chapter (N18), according to the chapter 14 coding document.\n\n**What the sources do not cover.** These documents explain how codes are assigned. They do not explain the medical relationship between the two conditions in any particular person, and they do not say how often the link is true in practice. I could not find a source on whether a specific note's findings support the link. That is a question for the clinician who wrote the note.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
